WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.073 --> 00:00:02.490
SPEAKER: So far, we've talked
about how Kubeflow Pipelines

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:05.220
bring orchestration for
complex ML workflows,

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:08.580
giving you reproducible and
reliable experimentation.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.200
And in our last
episode, I showed you

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:11.977
a prediction model ready to go.

00:00:11.977 --> 00:00:13.560
Let's walk through
building a Kubeflow

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.054
Pipeline that uses this model.

00:00:16.054 --> 00:00:19.540
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:22.450
Today, we'll go through a
sample ML app using Pipelines.

00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:23.950
Check out the link
below if you want

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:26.170
to read more about building
Pipelines in a Jupyter

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:27.130
notebook.

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:29.860
I'll use the same model from
the last episode, where you'll

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:32.170
forecast how many Chicago
crimes are expected

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:35.350
to be reported the next day
based on how many were reported

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:36.880
over the previous days.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:39.250
If you want to see more
details on the model itself,

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:41.080
check out the last video.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:43.330
To create an ML app
on Kubeflow, we'll

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:46.450
walk through downloading
data from BigQuery,

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:49.780
creating a Kubeflow Pipeline,
including Google Cloud AI

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:52.720
platform components to
train and deploy the model,

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:55.480
submitting a job for
execution, and sharing

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:57.170
the Pipeline with others.

00:00:57.170 --> 00:00:58.990
AI platform is
Google Cloud solution

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:02.410
to train ML models at scale,
host your trained models,

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:05.115
and serve models to
make new predictions.

00:01:05.115 --> 00:01:07.240
We're going to run this
all from a Jupyter notebook

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:09.860
server running on Kubeflow.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:12.650
On a running notebook, clone
the GitHub repo linked below

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:14.870
and access the AI
platform notebook.

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:16.760
If you run into any
authentication issues

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:18.620
while running the
notebook, make sure you're

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:20.450
on the latest
version of Kubeflow

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:25.470
and check out the documentation
around the user GCPSA secret.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:27.130
Getting started with
the notebook code

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:29.790
will import dependencies
and define constants,

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:32.070
including the Kubeflow SDK.

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:34.000
As you begin to
create your Pipeline,

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.700
first define the
required parameters

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.880
like the Google Cloud project
ID and storage bucket.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:40.920
Optional parameters
include your storage path

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:44.340
containing your untrained model,
the output storage bucket,

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.740
Pipeline name, and model name.

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:49.790
Similar to the Chicago crimes
notebook we covered last time,

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:51.540
start by defining a
download function that

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:54.990
will download trainer data
from Google Cloud BigQuery.

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:57.780
This time, you'll be using
the BigQuery Kubeflow Pipeline

00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:59.080
component.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:02.520
Remember that components include
inputs and outputs, which

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.160
can become inputs
to other components.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:07.620
This component submits a
query to the BigQuery surface

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:10.949
and dumps output to our
cloud storage bucket.

00:02:10.949 --> 00:02:13.740
Next, preprocess the data
and submit a training job

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:15.150
to AI platform.

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:18.900
Remember each step in a Pipeline
is a containerized task.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:20.430
This means that
every component must

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.590
be packaged as a container.

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:24.690
To do this, you can load
a Pipeline component

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:28.860
via a local file or a component
URL, like a GitHub commit,

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:32.590
making sharing versions,
and reuse easy.

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:35.020
In this example,
we call a YAML file

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:37.750
that defines our next component,
which submits a training

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:39.518
job to AI platform.

00:02:39.518 --> 00:02:41.560
While this is one way to
package a component that

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:43.720
makes sharing easier,
there are other ways

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:46.360
to do this, like using
the Pipelines SDK, which

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:48.610
includes classes and
methods for interacting

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:50.445
with Pipeline components.

00:02:50.445 --> 00:02:52.570
Inputs for this component
include the cloud storage

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:54.850
location of our model,
the input parameters

00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:57.340
to create a training job,
and the container image

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:59.920
URI to run this component.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:04.760
It outputs a job ID and storage
path to the trained model.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:07.030
The next component will
deploy the model with the ID

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:08.950
form our last output.

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:10.990
This YAML file describes
a Pipeline component

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:13.450
to deploy our trained
model from cloud storage

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:15.730
to an AI platform service.

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:18.700
Inputs include the cloud storage
path of the trained model,

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:22.890
and it outputs the storage path
containing our deployed model.

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:25.380
Now you can string together
all of these components

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:27.740
to define the Kubeflow
Pipeline-- just

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:31.800
define the data set storage path
and trainer output storage path

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:35.040
and call the download data
function, training function,

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.120
and model deployment function.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:40.000
Finally, submit the
Pipeline for execution.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.150
The Kubeflow Pipeline system
requires an experiment

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:43.680
to group Pipeline runs.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.180
If you don't specify
an experiment,

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:46.800
it will use the default one.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:48.540
It outputs a link to
both the experiment

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:50.950
and the run on Kubeflow.

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:53.210
Once you finish waiting for
the Pipeline to complete,

00:03:53.210 --> 00:03:54.835
you can see it in
the Kubeflow console,

00:03:54.835 --> 00:03:57.160
plus related logs from the run.

00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:00.080
Here we've added a line of
code to compile our Pipeline,

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:01.488
so we can share it with others.

00:04:01.488 --> 00:04:04.030
Once you run it, you can locate
the zip file in your notebook

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:05.920
server and download it.

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:07.970
The archive file can
be shared with others,

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:10.720
which lets them run it on
their own Kubeflow clusters.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:13.720
You can upload a Pipeline file
to the Kubeflow Pipelines page

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:16.300
with versioning
control built in.

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:19.260
Once uploaded, you can
create runs from the same UI.

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:22.120
And Kubeflow has a form to
fill out input parameters,

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:25.390
so you can start runs right
away, giving you reusability

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:27.310
at your fingertips.

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:29.670
Once you've deployed the
Pipeline and tested it,

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:32.170
you can share the archive files,
so your teams can deploy it

00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:33.760
on the production cluster.

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:35.770
In addition to sharing
the whole Pipeline,

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:37.660
you can also share
the component files,

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:40.270
making steps shareable
and reusable.

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:43.046
Stay tuned to learn where
else you can run Pipelines.

00:04:43.046 --> 00:04:47.768
[MUSIC PLAYING]

